---
description: How to build, run and test Tunnerer (C# / .NET 10) on Windows
alwaysApply: true
---

# Build, run, test

This branch is the C# game. The original C++ engine is on the `cplusplus-legacy` branch only; nothing here builds
with MSBuild/vcxproj or CMake.

```
dotnet build Tunnerer.slnx
dotnet test Tunnerer.slnx
dotnet run --project src/Tunnerer.Desktop -- --windowed
```

- Target framework: net10.0. Solution file: `Tunnerer.slnx`.
- `Tunnerer.Desktop` compiles its HLSL shaders with `fxc.exe` (Windows SDK) before each build. Override the path
  with `-p:Dx11ShaderCompiler=<path>`.
- `Tunnerer.Core` must not reference SDL, ImGui or Direct3D (`ArchitectureGuardTests` enforces it).
- The simulation must stay deterministic for a given seed: no `DateTime`, `Environment.TickCount` or unseeded
  `Random` in `Tunnerer.Core`.

# Running without disturbing the user

Use `--hidden --windowed=1280x720` for automated runs; the window is never shown. Drive the game with
`TUNNERER_SCRIPTED_INPUT`, `TUNNERER_COMMAND_SCRIPT` and `TUNNERER_SCRIPT_SCREENSHOT_FRAMES` (see `README.md`).
Screenshots are written as `.ppm` to `screenshots/` next to the executable.

# Where things are

- Gameplay constants: `src/Tunnerer.Core/Config/Tweaks.cs`
- Simulation step order: `World.Advance` in `src/Tunnerer.Core/World.cs`
- Game loop: `GameFrameCoordinator`, `Game.cs`; pause / controls / seed: `Game.Session.cs`
- HUD: `src/Tunnerer.Desktop/Gui/`
